  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">British Council Official Development Assistance (ODA)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The British Council supports peace and prosperity by building connections, understanding and trust between people in the UK and countries worldwide. All its ODA funded work, via its Global Programmes, supports this mission, in low-and middle-income countries, combining its deep expertise in arts and culture, education and the English language. This IATI activity entry is the parent-entry for its current set of Global Programmes funded by the ODA portion of its FCDO Grant-in-Aid. The British Council receives Grant-in-Aid from, and is sponsored by, the Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO), from which the British Council has operational independence as an Arms-Length Body. The relationship between the British Council and the FCDO is set out in the Framework Document, linked below.  The majority of the grant-in-aid received from the FCDO is required to be spent on Official Development Assistance (ODA) eligible activity. This has informed the purpose and outcomes of the Global Programmes. Note that for financial reporting: budget and finances are ODA only and are reported at a Global Programme level as opposed to this parent-level entry (see linked activities below).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The British Council supports peace and prosperity by building connections, understanding and trust between people in the UK and countries worldwide. All its ODA funded work, via its Global Programmes, supports this mission, in low-and middle-income countries, combining its deep expertise in arts and culture, education and the English language. This IATI activity entry is the parent-entry for its current set of Global Programmes funded by the ODA portion of its FCDO Grant-in-Aid. The British Council works directly with individuals to help them gain the skills, confidence and connections to transform their lives and shape a better world in partnership with the UK. It supports them to build networks and explore creative ideas, to get a high-quality education and to gain internationally recognised qualifications. The British Council supports leaders in overseas governments, institutions and organisations as a long-term and trustworthy partner committed to achieving mutual benefit. Working together it makes a bigger difference, creating benefit for millions of people all over the world. The British Council&#x2019;s Global Programmes contribute to:&#x25FC; 1.Strengthening creative and economic development&#x25FC; 2.Enriching the quality of education&#x25FC; 3.Improving access to the English language&#x25FC; 4.Addressing global challenges including improving gender equality, reducing inequalities and supporting action on climate change</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The British Council&#x2019;s Global Programmes target and engage with different audiences globally and in the UK. Please refer to individual Global Programme entries for the specific audience targeted by the activity.</narrative>
  </description>
